WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:06.050
Consider the following game.

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:09.400
Eve instructs Bob
to go into a room.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.960
Bob finds the room empty, except
for some locks, an empty box,

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.580
and a single deck of cards.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:19.560
Eve tells Bob to select
a card from the deck

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.015
and hide it as best he can.

00:00:23.015 --> 00:00:24.800
The rules are simple.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.980
Bob cannot leave the
room with anything.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.230
Cards and keys all
stay in the room.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:34.900
And he can put, at most,
one card in the box.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.330
Eve agrees that she has
never seen the locks.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:42.800
He wins the game if Eve is
unable to determine his card.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.110
So what is his best strategy?

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:48.090
Well, Bob selected a
card, 6 of diamonds

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:50.690
and threw it in the box.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:53.530
First, he considered the
different types of locks.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:58.540
Maybe he should lock the
card in the box with the key.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.160
Though, she could pick locks.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.020
So he considers the
combination lock.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:04.260
The key is on the back.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.310
So if he locks it
and scratches it off,

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:09.180
it seems like the best choice.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.990
But suddenly, he
realizes the problem.

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:14.790
The remaining cards on
the table leak information

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:18.330
about his choice, since it's
now missing from the deck.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.465
The locks are a decoy.

00:01:20.465 --> 00:01:23.900
He shouldn't separate
his card from the deck.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.740
He returns his card
to the deck but can't

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.110
remember the
position of his card.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.230
So shuffles the deck
to randomize it.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.520
Shuffling is the
best lock because it

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:37.710
leaves no information
about his choice.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:42.150
His card is now equally likely
to be any card in the deck.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:48.150
He can now leave the cards
openly in confidence.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:51.060
Bob wins the game because
the best Eve can do

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:55.090
is simply guess as he
has left no information

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:56.950
about his choice.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.220
Most importantly,
even if we give Eve

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.690
unlimited computational
power, she

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.090
can't do any better
than a guess.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:08.729
This defines what we
call perfect secrecy.

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:13.580
On September 1, 1945,
29-year-old Claude Shannon

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:17.430
published a classified
paper on this idea.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:20.210
Shannon gave the first
mathematical proof

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:25.440
for how and why the one-time
pad is perfectly secret.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.820
Shannon thinks about encryption
schemes in the following way.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:33.780
Imagine Alice writes a message
to Bob 20 letters long.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:38.060
This is equivalent to
picking one specific page

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:39.990
from the message space.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:43.670
The message space can be thought
of as a complete collection

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:47.680
of all possible
20-letter messages.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.170
Anything you can think of
that's 20 letters long,

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.570
is a page in this stack.

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:55.330
Next, Alice applies
a shared key which

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:58.070
is a list of 20
randomly-generated shifts

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.290
between 1 and 26.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.850
The key space is the
complete collection

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.170
of all possible outcomes.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:08.340
So generating a key is
equivalent to selecting a page

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.650
from this stack at random.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.700
When she applies the shifts
to encrypt the message,

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.620
she ends up with a ciphertext.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.480
The ciphertext space
represents all possible results

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.830
of an encryption.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:26.820
When she applies the key,
it maps to a unique page

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:28.510
in this stack.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:30.740
Notice that the size
of the message space

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.880
equals the size of
the key space equals

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.820
the size of the
ciphertext space.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.130
This defines what we
call perfect secrecy.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:42.612
For if someone has access to
a page of ciphertext only,

00:03:42.612 --> 00:03:46.150
the only thing that they
know is that every message

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.420
is equally likely.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.580
So no amount of
computational power

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:54.000
could ever help
improve a blind guess.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.805
Now, the big problem, you're
wondering, with a one-time pad

00:03:56.805 --> 00:04:00.100
is we have to share these
long keys in advance.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.380
To solve this problem, we
need to relax our definition

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:08.360
of secrecy by developing a
definition of pseudorandomness.

